narrative:
## Counting Anything

A **Counter** tracks how many times you've seen each item. With generics, one `Counter[T]` works for strings, numbers, or any type.

### Defining a Generic Counter

```xiom
type Counter[T] = {
  items: Vec[T];
}

fn Counter.new[T]() -> Counter[T] {
  return Counter[T]{ items: Vec[T].new() };
}

fn Counter.add[T](self: &mut Counter[T], item: T) {
  self.items.push(item);
}

fn Counter.count[T](self) -> Int {
  return self.items.len();
}

fn Counter.clear[T](self: &mut Counter[T]) {
  while self.items.len() > 0 {
    self.items.pop();
  }
}
```

### Using the Counter

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var visits: Counter[Str] = Counter[Str].new();
  visits.add("homepage");
  visits.add("about");
  visits.add("homepage");
  visits.add("contact");

  io.println("Total visits: " + string.int_to_str(visits.count()));

  visits.clear();
  io.println("After clear: " + string.int_to_str(visits.count()));
  return 0;
}
```

### Different Types, Same Counter

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var str_counter: Counter[Str] = Counter[Str].new();
  str_counter.add("login");
  str_counter.add("logout");
  io.println(string.int_to_str(str_counter.count()));

  var int_counter: Counter[Int] = Counter[Int].new();
  int_counter.add(42);
  int_counter.add(7);
  io.println(string.int_to_str(int_counter.count()));

  return 0;
}
```

### Listing All Items

```xiom
fn Counter.list_all[T](self) -> Vec[T] {
  let result: Vec[T] = Vec[T].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < self.items.len() {
    let item = match self.items.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    result.push(item);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

Notice: we never care what T is. The Counter works whether T is Int, Str, Bool, or any custom type you create.

